WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.222
OT recovery requires more than server backups.

00:00:04.222 --> 00:00:19.373
Preserve controller logic, safety and control configurations, HMI applications, engineering projects, network and security-device settings, firmware references, licenses, historian definitions, recipes, and the data required by the recovery objective.

00:00:19.373 --> 00:00:30.549
System images can accelerate recovery for HMIs, engineering workstations, and servers, but hardware, drivers, vendor software, and activation requirements must match.

00:00:30.549 --> 00:00:37.255
Record the operating system, application version, patches, device interfaces, and restoration sequence.

00:00:37.255 --> 00:00:43.464
Keep protected copies offline or otherwise separated from ordinary administrative access.

00:00:43.464 --> 00:00:49.674
Use access control, integrity checks, encryption where appropriate, and multiple locations.

00:00:49.674 --> 00:00:57.373
Ransomware resilience depends on backups that the compromised environment cannot easily alter or delete.

00:00:57.373 --> 00:01:02.589
Restore testing is the proof that a backup works.

00:01:02.589 --> 00:01:11.282
Use a lab, spare device, test environment, or vendor-supported process where production restoration would be unsafe.

00:01:11.282 --> 00:01:20.471
Verify not only that files open, but that logic, communications, alarms, licenses, and dependent services function together.

00:01:20.471 --> 00:01:23.700
Vendor-specific requirements can determine success.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:30.903
Some systems require exact firmware, proprietary cables, hardware keys, certificates, or approved procedures.

00:01:30.903 --> 00:01:37.609
Maintain recovery kits, compatible spares, software media, credentials, and current support contacts.

00:01:37.609 --> 00:01:44.315
Track backup age, scope, owner, storage location, protection, and last restore test.

00:01:44.315 --> 00:01:50.027
After every approved change, confirm that recoverable copies were updated.

00:01:50.027 --> 00:02:03.191
A backup inventory should map directly to critical processes and recovery sequences, ensuring the organization can restore a known-good operational state rather than isolated files.
